    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d246">

                <pb facs="0325" n="315" sameAs="frus:frus1943China/pg315"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/15108</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Atcheson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1480</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-08-17">August 17,
                        1943</date>.<lb/>[Received September 3.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the Embassy’s
                    telegrams Nos. 1325, July 28, 6 p.m. and 1438, August 12, 6 p.m., and to
                    enclose, as of possible background interest, a copy of a memorandum of August 3,
                        1943<anchor n="32" xml:id="d246-f1"/> from the Embassy officer at Lanchow in
                    which various illustrations are given of the antipathy to Soviet Russia and the
                    Chinese Communists currently being manifested in official and Kuomintang Party
                    circles in Lanchow.</p>
                <p>These illustrations include the onerous restrictions upon the activities and
                    contacts of the Soviet Embassy representative at Lanchow; the minimizing, in
                    local propaganda, of Russia’s part in the war; various evidences of Chinese
                    expectations of a future conflict with <pb facs="0326" n="316" xml:id="pg316"/>
                    Russia; and remarks of various officials indicating antagonism toward Soviet
                    Russia and the Chinese Communists.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">George Atcheson, Jr.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
